00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:38.620
The camera perspective doesn't make it harder nor easier to do this minigame.

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:40.860
And so therefore, I question why you move around at all.

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:44.800
However, they do work it into the timing thing of, like, how much time you have.

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:49.400
Because once you release the ball, you have to go, you know, run over to the next area.

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:53.900
So I like that. You know, there's a little psychology to it, if you will.

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:59.540
Yeah. But other than that, yeah, I didn't really feel like there was any...

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:08.540
I'm sure there is, but I could not get the feel for what is a good release time for these.

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:13.520
It just seemed like I was doing the exact same timing, and it was...

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:15.240
I was getting different results.

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:20.440
Yeah. I also felt that way, you know, throughout a lot of the game.

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.120
But that was actually a question I had.

00:34:22.120 --> 00:34:25.740
I was curious to what you guys thought about, you know, with games like this.

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:29.460
Like, I did notice once in a while that it felt like there were different release times.

00:34:29.680 --> 00:34:34.040
Maybe not in this mode, but, like, depending on where I was on the court, different release times that were important.

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:41.080
But it never felt enough, like, to really make me feel like it was anything other than, like, shoot the ball.

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:43.460
And, you know, maybe it'll go in and maybe it won't.

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:50.820
Like, it doesn't feel like there's any of my skill or lack thereof that's translating to whether or not I hit the basket.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:57.900
And that's kind of something I never really fully understand about basketball video games in general.

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:06.400
Like, I've never really played many of them, so I was wondering if you guys have and know of the more modern ones.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:13.140
Where, like, what... I mean, the main element of basketball is making a basket.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:23.600
And if that is reduced to just getting open and making a shot and hoping that, like, the RNG gives you a good shot,

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:26.280
that, to me, seems like it's missing something.

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:34.060
Yeah. Like, so with more modern basketball games, there's a lot more, like, visual cues that can tell you, like,

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:38.480
how you can adjust your shot after you miss.

00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:44.500
Like, you can see that, like, oh, I hit the backboard, like, super hard and it just bounced out.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:46.560
Like, I'll have to take a little bit off of it.

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:48.480
Or you'll just miss entirely.

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:55.000
But other than that, there's usually, like, a visual, like, gauge.

00:35:55.800 --> 00:36:01.280
And, like, the timing will change how much of the gauge you fill.

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:09.540
And then each player has a different, like, sort of tolerance for how well you need to shoot to actually make it a good shot,

00:36:09.680 --> 00:36:14.040
with better players having a wider net for that.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:20.040
And then there's also just, like, is anybody, like, hanging on top of me?

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:24.580
Because you're obviously going to shoot better when you don't have a guy in your face.

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:32.560
So in more modern games, there's a lot more that you can base your next move on.

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:33.760
Like, there's more information.

00:36:33.940 --> 00:36:39.080
Whereas this, like, you either miss a little bit to the left or a little bit to the right, or you go straight in.

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:48.360
Yeah. Okay. I get that now, especially with the more modern stuff, that that level of complexity feels like that you can get, like, actually a feel for it,

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:51.960
where it'll start to feel like basketball.

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:57.660
I just never fully get some of these older games, even some, like, hockey games and stuff.

00:36:57.780 --> 00:37:00.440
I mean, I know there's a lot more to these sports than just shooting.

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:07.400
But, like, if, like, basketball, such a major component of playing basketball, is aiming,

00:37:08.220 --> 00:37:11.940
that skill in these older games just does not translate to me at all.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:14.800
That's why I, you know, there must be something I'm missing,

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:20.500
because people who, a lot of people who like basketball and like sports games liked these games.

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:27.660
So for me, I'm just, I'm just confused on the appeal of, like, a shootout mode in one of these games.

00:37:28.280 --> 00:37:30.060
Well, I think it's two things first.

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:35.720
You know, it's like, they're NBA players, so we're expecting them to make most of the shots, right?

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:39.600
Like, you know, I'm not saying that they should make every shot guaranteed, because that'd be really boring.

00:37:39.780 --> 00:37:44.420
But, like, we're also not expecting them to, you know, miss layups for the most part or stuff like that.

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:47.940
So, you know, these things, you know, there's an expectation there.

00:37:48.200 --> 00:37:54.540
But, you know, I'm going to say that I actually did feel like the release time mattered on this,

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:55.860
like where you let go of the ball.

00:37:55.860 --> 00:38:02.620
I do feel like if you're at the, like, top of your jump and you release the button,

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:06.800
I did feel like more times I was making the shot than not making it.

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:08.460
And that could be entirely in my head.

00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:13.620
But if you release that ball, like, early on in the jump, you're guaranteed to miss it.

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:18.640
And if you do it after you're on your way down, you're also pretty much guaranteed to miss it.

00:38:18.680 --> 00:38:23.660
So I did feel like, you know, maybe it was a little too precise where you feel like, you know,

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:26.480
you're in the top of your jump and you're still missing it.

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:30.940
Or maybe they do program it to, like, even if you are perfect, you're still going to miss it.

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:32.140
Like, which I think would be a mistake.

00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:33.300
But maybe that's what they did.

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:40.380
Overall, though, I do feel that as long as, you know, there's some kind of built-in mechanic there

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:44.840
that, like, the arc of your jump and where you release, if that actually does matter,

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:49.380
which I personally felt like it did, that's enough for me in these older games.

00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:51.420
You know, Double Dribble had that, too, if you remember.

00:38:51.540 --> 00:38:54.280
You know, it was about where you release the ball on your jump.

00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:56.260
As long as they have that, that's enough for me here.

00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:59.440
And I think that's, like, the kind of enjoyment people would have gotten back then

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:01.740
or even just now in these style of games.

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:07.260
However, the three-point shootout is still just like a, you know,

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:11.060
there's no real reward for this or the slam dunk contest.

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:13.980
Like, it just, you just keep playing on, I suppose.

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:19.240
And so these things all just eventually just feel like warm-ups for the true one-on-one mode

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:20.840
where it's Larry versus Michael.

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:28.720
Yeah, even though, like, in the slam dunk, they change how the game works.

00:39:28.900 --> 00:39:31.240
But other than that, I agree.

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:36.520
Yeah, it would have been cool if they would have taken the slam dunk stuff

00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:39.380
and somehow worked it into the one-on-one, you know?

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:41.320
Like, I'm sure it would have just been exclusive to Michael,

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.460
but, you know, for that reason, they probably didn't consider it.

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:46.820
But, like, if there were actual, like, cool inputs to do,

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:48.940
it'd be fun to see them make fun dunks.

00:39:49.040 --> 00:39:53.040
Like, you know, the appeal of NBA Jam and stuff like that is the flair.

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:54.720
There's no flair in this game.

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:56.200
It's a pretty boring game.

00:39:56.200 --> 00:39:59.220
It's got, yeah, it has no style.

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:00.760
No style at all.

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:04.940
And the whole presentation is like that.

00:40:05.060 --> 00:40:08.540
The only style in the game is when you do dunks.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:13.160
Yeah, and as far as I'm concerned, there are no dunks in this game.

00:40:14.960 --> 00:40:18.320
So, yeah, for me, I feel like the only appeal that I could see

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:21.740
that puts this over other basketball games is Larry Bird and Michael Jordan.

00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:23.480
Michael and Larry Legend.

00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:24.960
Oh, right, yeah, Mike and Larry.

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:26.400
Mike or Michael? Michael.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:28.960
Mikey. And then Larry.

00:40:29.680 --> 00:40:33.780
Larry Legend. It's Michael and Like Mike.

00:40:34.100 --> 00:40:35.640
That feels unbalanced, too, though.

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:36.600
We need to give...

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:38.020
Is that Lil' Bow Wow?

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:39.340
Yeah. Lil' Bow Wow.

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:43.100
Yeah, we need to give Michael Jordan, like, you know, a nickname.

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:46.840
I think the manual does a good job of singing both of their praises

00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:48.260
differently and equally.

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:50.340
You know, where it's like, oh, he's good at this,

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:52.940
and he's good at this, and they both feel like, oh, who am I going to pick?

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:58.220
Well, we all know that, like, in the 80s, like, Michael Jordan's nickname

00:40:58.220 --> 00:40:59.540
was just Jumpin' Jordan.

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:03.380
There you go. We all know that.

00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:05.280
Of course I know that.

00:41:05.660 --> 00:41:12.860
Yeah. Would you believe me if I said that this game is a sequel?

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:15.640
No. You liar.

00:41:15.640 --> 00:41:20.020
Well, you're going to have to, because the original game is...

00:41:20.020 --> 00:41:21.640
See, they did something clever here.

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:26.120
In this game, it's Michael Jordan versus Larry Bird.

00:41:26.200 --> 00:41:27.220
What is this game called again?

00:41:27.340 --> 00:41:28.240
Jordan versus Bird.

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:30.160
One-on-one. Right.

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:33.940
Now, they flipped it, and the original game is one-on-one,

00:41:34.500 --> 00:41:36.700
Dr. J versus Larry Bird.

00:41:37.360 --> 00:41:39.060
Oh. I see.

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:43.580
So, Dr. J, you know, who I guess could be Michael Jordan's nickname, too,

00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:47.580
right? Like, I know it's been taken, but theoretically, he could be another Dr.

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:48.740
J if he got a PhD.

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:51.120
It's not him.

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:53.020
I understand. Absolutely.

00:41:53.780 --> 00:41:56.620
Of course. But since we were fishing for nicknames, I thought I'd give it a shot.

00:41:56.920 --> 00:41:58.780
Anyway, that was a computer game.

00:41:58.960 --> 00:42:01.960
It never came to any consoles or anything like that, but that was a computer game.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:06.600
And I guess it did well enough that Electronic Arts was like, yeah, let's make

00:42:06.600 --> 00:42:08.500
another one, but release it for more consoles.

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:12.100
So, that's how you get Jordan versus Bird here.

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:18.580
And Larry Bird obviously is in, like, legacy editions of the NBA, like, 2K

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:23.060
games and stuff like that, where they start to include, like, great basketball

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:27.340
players. But he never got, like, his own game, and I feel like that's a shame.

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:30.920
But Michael Jordan obviously does get some of his own games.

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:36.960
We have Michael Jordan in Flight, which is, believe it or not, that is the title

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:38.480
of the game. It came out in 1993.

00:42:38.760 --> 00:42:40.120
It's a flight sim. Only for computers.

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:42.540
It's a flight sim. Only for computers.

00:42:43.300 --> 00:42:44.740
And it's not a flight simulator.

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:46.460
That would be funny, though.

00:42:46.480 --> 00:42:48.540
I could imagine that, you know, the misdirection.

00:42:48.740 --> 00:42:51.720
You just play as Michael Jordan, like, flying to L.A.

00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:53.920
for a game. He's just a passenger.

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:55.340
Oh, as a passenger.

00:42:55.480 --> 00:42:59.240
Okay. But it's a game with only Michael Jordan, if you can believe that.

00:42:59.300 --> 00:43:04.760
There's no NBA license players or teams otherwise, so it's just Michael, and it's

00:43:04.760 --> 00:43:09.500
a three-on-three basketball match, but one of them has Michael Jordan.

00:43:09.700 --> 00:43:11.740
Oh, okay. So there are other players.

00:43:11.980 --> 00:43:13.960
That's not very fair.

00:43:13.960 --> 00:43:17.860
That could have worked as if they were all Michael Jordan, and these games just have a problem

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:19.700
with, like, having clones.

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:26.220
I thought that they were looking at this game, and they're like, oh, they did two players?

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:27.120
We'll do even one better.

00:43:27.220 --> 00:43:28.200
We'll just do one player.

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:29.920
Only one player playing basketball.

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:31.020
Just free throws.

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:36.920
The problem is, is that Michael understood, like, you know, merchandising and licensing

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:42.340
and stuff like that, so he actually never allowed himself to be in any of those NBA games

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:43.480
with every other player.

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:48.460
So, you know, if you look at, like, NBA Jam, you famously cannot pick Michael Jordan because

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:54.080
he wouldn't allow his likeliness in the game, but Bill Clinton and Hillary Clinton are in

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:54.880
NBA Jam.

00:43:54.880 --> 00:43:57.020
So did they sign off on that?

00:43:57.100 --> 00:44:00.140
Like, did they write in to the president and be like, we want to put you in our game?

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:01.600
Which is weird, because I guess that...

00:44:01.600 --> 00:44:04.440
I don't know. Does that mean that he just wasn't a part of the Players Association?

00:44:04.680 --> 00:44:05.720
Because it's usually...

00:44:06.080 --> 00:44:09.300
I mean, now we're just getting into the weeds of sports games, but it's like, usually you

00:44:09.300 --> 00:44:13.520
have to sign a contract with the league and then with the Players Association for their

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:16.460
likenesses. So I don't know how that worked.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:20.640
I'm pretty confident I can dig up an article on this and put it in the show notes.

00:44:20.820 --> 00:44:22.020
So let's make a note of that.

00:44:22.180 --> 00:44:23.240
I'll put it in the show notes. Do it.

